// A program whose only route to TLS is reflection is not linked against
// OpenSSL, and the call that does arrive at a TLS helper is a named failure.
//
// This is W9's rule stated as a program. Neither of the two calls below names a
// TLS method, and no live call site in the generated C reaches ty_tls_*: what
// reaches it is the member table reflection reads, whose invoker calls the
// method body -- Tls.clientContext, whose own body calls the native helper.
// (A native method cannot be reached this way at all: reflection runs the
// body the prelude writes, which answers that it is implemented in C.) Before the rule was implemented the reflection metadata made the
// whole TLS layer reachable, so this program was built with
// internal/runtime/src/tyrt_tls.c and linked against -lssl -- on a machine
// without the headers the build failed outright -- and the call below ran the
// real OpenSSL code, answering with the handle of a context built from the
// system's trust anchors.
//
// What it must get instead is the runtime's weak stub
// (internal/runtime/src/tyrt_net.c): a named UnsupportedOperationException,
// thrown at the call rather than inside a slot the pruner emptied. The message
// names the Teyru method, which is the name a reader can search for, and says
// nothing about the machine: OpenSSL may be installed and the program simply
// never named it.
//
// The expected output is this program's, not the JDK's: `teyru emit-java`
// refuses the reflection layer by name (java.lang.reflect has a different API),
// so the differential says nothing about it.

import java.lang.reflect.*

class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Class tls = Class.forName("teyru.Tls")
    Method context = tls.getMethod("clientContext", String.class)
    try {
      Object handle = context.invoke(null, "")
      System.out.println("invoked=" + handle)
    } catch (InvocationTargetException e) {
      System.out.println("cause=" + e.getCause().getClass().getName())
      System.out.println("message=" + e.getCause().getMessage())
    }
  }
}
